WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.806
Conditional Access replaces the
logon restrictions and network

2
00:00:03.806 --> 00:00:07.425
controls that Active Directory
enforced through Group Policy.

3
00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:13.113
The program organizes it around personas:
populations with distinct needs and risk.

4
00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:18.025
Internals are employees and contractors,
identified by the worker type code.

5
00:00:18.025 --> 00:00:21.931
Admins are users eligible for
or holding privileged roles,

6
00:00:21.931 --> 00:00:27.192
identified by account type or a group of
PIM-eligible users; target them by group,

7
00:00:27.192 --> 00:00:31.417
because role targeting supports
built-in directory roles only,

8
00:00:31.417 --> 00:00:33.250
not AU-scoped or custom ones.

9
00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:38.403
Externals are guests from partner organizations,
Guests are ad hoc external users,

10
00:00:38.403 --> 00:00:41.988
Service accounts are
single-tenant workload identities,

11
00:00:41.988 --> 00:00:45.050
and Break-glass is the
dedicated exclusion group.

12
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:50.075
The baseline set runs
from CA001 through CA017.

13
00:00:50.075 --> 00:00:51.287
A few anchors.

14
00:00:51.287 --> 00:00:56.113
CA001 blocks legacy
authentication for all users.

15
00:00:56.113 --> 00:01:01.521
CA003 requires the phishing-resistant
authentication strength for admins,

16
00:01:01.521 --> 00:01:03.887
with a four-hour sign-in frequency.

17
00:01:03.887 --> 00:01:10.563
CA006 requires MFA for internals,
phishing-resistant as the end state.

18
00:01:10.563 --> 00:01:15.112
CA008 blocks internals
outside trusted countries.

19
00:01:15.112 --> 00:01:21.668
CA013 uses Microsoft's newer require risk
remediation control for high user risk;

20
00:01:21.668 --> 00:01:27.550
it isn't supported for guests, so CA012
blocks risky guest sign-ins instead.

21
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:32.638
CA015 blocks workload identities
outside trusted networks.

22
00:01:32.638 --> 00:01:37.850
CA002, the old break-glass grant
policy, is retired: a policy can't

23
00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:43.963
create an exemption; the exclusion on
every other policy is the exemption.

24
00:01:43.963 --> 00:01:49.625
Appendix C carries Graph JSON templates,
all created in report-only state.

25
00:01:49.625 --> 00:01:54.129
Book 04's library is
authoritative and adds CA018

26
00:01:54.129 --> 00:01:59.512
through CA038 for the
CISA BOD 25-01 baseline.

27
00:01:59.512 --> 00:02:04.965
Authentication strengths name what's
accepted: phishing-resistant MFA is Windows

28
00:02:04.965 --> 00:02:10.813
Hello for Business, FIDO2 passkeys, or
multifactor certificate-based authentication.

29
00:02:10.813 --> 00:02:15.224
Authenticator push and phone sign-in
aren't phishing-resistant on their own.

30
00:02:15.224 --> 00:02:20.266
Rollout has three stages: report-only
for two to four weeks, a two-week pilot,

31
00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:25.225
then phased waves over four to eight
weeks, each behind a monitoring gate.

32
00:02:25.225 --> 00:02:28.738
And never switch an all-users
policy straight from off to on.

33
00:02:28.738 --> 00:02:33.980
Confirm the break-glass exclusion, and that
both emergency accounts still sign in.
